    "Perpendicular body alignment maintained in T-shape relative to opponent, with your shoulders and upper body positioned at roughly 90 degrees to their torso, your chest facing toward their trapped leg while your head is positioned away from their free leg to avoid counters",
    "Hip elevation maintained for pressure with your hips slightly raised off the mat to create constant downward force on opponent's trapped leg and hip, using your core engagement to generate continuous control pressure that exhausts their defensive structure",
    "Upper body control established through grips on opponent's collar, sleeve, or direct body contact to prevent them from sitting up or creating defensive frames, maintaining the fundamental rule that they must remain flat on their back for the position to function optimally"
